[38;2;124;124;124mA Hilbert space H is isomorphic (as a Hilbert space, i.e., unitarily equivalent)[0m[38;2;124;124;124mto L^2(μ) for some measure space (X, M, μ).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mKey facts:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- Every Hilbert space has an orthonormal basis (by Zorn's lemma). Let the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mdimension (cardinality of an orthonormal basis) be κ.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- L^2(μ) spaces: For a measure space (X, M, μ), L^2(μ) is a Hilbert space. Its [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mdimension depends on the measure space.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe question is whether for every cardinal κ, there's a measure space whose L^2 [0m[38;2;124;124;124mhas dimension κ.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor finite κ = n: Take X = {1,...,n} with counting measure. L^2 = ℂ^n. ✓[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor κ = ℵ_0 (separable infinite-dimensional): Take X = ℕ with counting measure. [0m[38;2;124;124;124mL^2 = ℓ^2. ✓[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor uncountable κ: Take X a set of cardinality κ with counting measure. Then [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mL^2(X, counting) = ℓ^2(X), the space of functions f: X → ℂ with Σ|f(x)|^2 < ∞, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mwhich means f has countable support. The orthonormal basis {δ_x : x ∈ X} has [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcardinality κ. So ℓ^2(X) has dimension κ. ✓[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo for any Hilbert space H with orthonormal basis of cardinality κ, H ≅ ℓ^2(κ) =[0m[38;2;124;124;124mL^2(X, P(X), counting measure) where |X| = κ.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis works for any cardinal κ. So yes, every Hilbert space is isomorphic to an [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mL^2 space.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWait, let me double check the counting measure case for uncountable sets. With [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcounting measure on an uncountable set X, L^2 consists of (equivalence classes [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mof) measurable functions f with ∫|f|^2 dμ < ∞. With counting measure, every [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfunction is measurable, and ∫|f|^2 dμ = Σ_{x∈X} |f(x)|^2. For this sum to be [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfinite, f must be nonzero on at most countably many points (since the sum of [0m
